{%- comment -%}
  Two doors — the split directly under the hero film. Built 2026-09-13,
  unplaced the same day in favour of `home-tabs.liquid`'s tab switch, then
  RE-PLACED 2026-09-21 as "What We Do" (see the comment further down for that
  history) with its original photograph-driven design redesigned the same day
  into the text-and-motion-only version below.

  WHAT THIS SECTION IS FOR, because it decides every choice below. The studio's
  own words: "tujuan website ini tuh utamanya bukan untuk jualan, tapi untuk
  biar wear label ini makin legit … intinya mau tekenin kalo ini tuh bisa untuk
  B2B". The site is a credibility surface first and a till second, and the thing
  it has never said loudly enough is that Wear Label produces custom apparel for
  organisations as well as trousers for shoppers. This band says both at once,
  in the first screenful after the film.

  IT IS TWO DOORS AND NOT A TAB STRIP, and that was argued out before it was
  built. A tab shows one side at a time, and the default tab would inevitably be
  ready-to-wear, because that is what most visitors arrive for — so a visitor who
  never thinks to click would never learn the second business exists. That is
  precisely the problem this band was commissioned to fix, moved somewhere else.
  Two panels side by side are read without being operated. They also need no
  script, have no state to keep in step, and survive a phone, where a tab strip
  would still be showing one side at a time.

  THE SPLIT IS 50/50, on instruction 2026-09-21 — "gw mau 50:50 gw gamau yg
  b2c lebih besar". Earlier this file argued for an uneven 58/42 split to
  correct for ready-to-wear having three sections of proof below this band
  against custom apparel's one; that argument assumed a photograph doing the
  weighting. With no photograph on either side, equal columns are the neutral
  choice and the studio asked for it explicitly.

  TONE IS A SETTING, NOT A POSITION, and every value has to be one that can sit
  next to another without the two reading as one band. Below `lg` the panels
  stack, and the repo's rule is that two bands of the same colour must not
  touch — so a build with both doors set to the same tone would read as one
  tall band with a seam in it. `custom-band` documents the same trap from the
  other direction.

  THE READY-TO-WEAR DOOR WAS ESPRESSO AND IS NOW `brand` (Camel, #75604F —
  effectively `--wl-taupe-600`, already the site's brand-action colour) —
  changed 2026-09-22, on instruction: "yg kiri hitam jd coklat". `bg-brand` /
  `text-on-brand` were reused rather than repointing `--color-invert`, which
  is the footer's and the voices wall's background too — retinting that token
  would have turned both of them brown as an unintended side effect of a
  change asked for on one panel. See `design/color-palette.jpg` and its entry
  in CLAUDE.md's Design sources for where this brown comes from.

  NO PHOTOGRAPH ON EITHER DOOR, since 2026-09-21 — see the comment on the
  panel markup below for why. `door-custom.webp` and `door-shop.webp` are no
  longer referenced anywhere in the theme as a result; they are not deleted,
  in case a future session wants the photograph version back.

  EACH PANEL IS ONE STRETCHED LINK, the pattern `product-card` uses: the anchor
  is on the heading and its `::after` covers the panel. A whole-panel `<a>` would
  have given the link an accessible name made of the heading, the sentence and
  the button label read out as one run-on string. This way the pointer gets the
  whole panel and a screen reader gets the heading.

  NO PRICE, NO PRODUCT COUNT AND NO CLIENT COUNT anywhere in here. The brief is
  explicit that the B2B side must not be inflated, and a number is the easiest
  way to inflate one.
{%- endcomment -%}

<section
  aria-labelledby="two-ways-heading"
  class="border-y border-rule"
>
  {%- comment -%}
    RE-PLACED 2026-09-21 with a visible intro above the two doors — "What We
    Do", asked for directly under the hero film. The sr-only-heading comment
    above no longer describes this section: `heading` now paints, and
    `aria-labelledby` still points at it because a real heading is a better
    landmark name than a hidden one.

    REDESIGNED THE SAME DAY, second pass — the photograph version read as
    "jelek" (ugly) once seen live. Asked to drop the eyebrow, drop both
    photographs entirely and carry the section on type and motion alone, and
    to split 50/50 rather than 58/42. The 58/42 argument further up this
    comment no longer applies: it was a photograph-driven case for uneven
    weight, and with no photograph on either side there is nothing left to
    balance — equal type deserves equal width.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if section.settings.heading != blank or section.settings.body != blank -%}
    <div data-reveal class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-content flex-col gap-4 px-gutter pt-section">
      <h2 id="two-ways-heading" class="text-h1 leading-h1">
        {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.heading, label: 'section heading' %}
      </h2>

      {%- if section.settings.body != blank -%}
        <p class="wl-measure text-body leading-body text-ink-muted">{{ section.settings.body }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 {% if section.settings.heading != blank or section.settings.body != blank %}mt-block{% endif %}">
    {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign tone = block.settings.tone

        if tone == 'invert'
          assign panel_bg = 'bg-invert'
          assign link_ink = 'text-ink-invert hover:text-ink-invert-muted'
          assign body_ink = 'text-ink-invert-muted'
          assign cta_ink = 'text-ink-invert'
        elsif tone == 'brand'
          assign panel_bg = 'bg-brand'
          assign link_ink = 'text-on-brand hover:text-canvas'
          assign body_ink = 'text-on-brand'
          assign cta_ink = 'text-on-brand'
        else
          assign panel_bg = 'bg-canvas'
          assign link_ink = 'text-ink hover:text-brand'
          assign body_ink = 'text-ink-muted'
          assign cta_ink = 'text-brand'
        endif

        assign door_href = block.settings.href | default: routes.all_products_collection_url
      -%}

      <div
        data-reveal
        class="group relative flex min-h-72 flex-col justify-center p-7 sm:p-10 lg:p-12 {{ panel_bg }} {% unless forloop.last %}border-b border-rule lg:border-b-0 lg:border-r{% endunless %}"
        {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
      >
        {%- comment -%}
          NO PHOTOGRAPH, ON PURPOSE, SECOND PASS. The photograph version of
          this panel was seen live and read as "jelek" (ugly) — asked to carry
          it on type and motion alone instead. `min-h-72` replaces the height
          the square photograph used to set, so the two panels still read as
          one band rather than collapsing to their text's own height; centring
          the content in that height is what stops it from looking like an
          empty box with a caption pinned to the top.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="flex w-full max-w-160 flex-col gap-4">
          {%- comment -%}
            THE COLOUR IS ON THE ANCHOR, NOT ON THE HEADING, and that is not a
            tidy-up. base.css gives a link its own colour, which wins over a
            colour inherited from the h3 — so with the class on the heading the
            espresso panel drew its headline in brand taupe, rgb(114,94,76) on
            rgb(30,26,22). It rendered, it passed every check, and it was
            unreadable. footer.liquid and snippets/button.liquid both document
            the same trap; this is the third place it has bitten.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <h3 class="text-h1 leading-h1">
            <a
              href="{{ door_href }}"
              class="transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) after:absolute after:inset-0 after:content-[''] {{ link_ink }}"
            >{% render 'copy', value: block.settings.heading, label: 'heading', inline: true %}</a>
          </h3>

          <p class="wl-measure text-body leading-body {{ body_ink }}">
            {% render 'copy', value: block.settings.body, label: 'description', lines: 2 %}
          </p>

          {%- comment -%}
            A span and not a button, and not a second anchor either: the panel
            is already one link, and an `<a>` inside an `<a>` is invalid markup
            that browsers repair by closing the outer one early. This is the
            affordance for the link that is already there — the only motion in
            the panel now that the photograph's hover-scale is gone.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if block.settings.cta != blank -%}
            <span class="mt-2 inline-flex items-center gap-2.5 text-label uppercase tracking-nav {{ cta_ink }}">
              {{ block.settings.cta }}
              <span class="inline-flex transition-transform duration-(--duration-base) ease-(--ease-entrance) motion-safe:group-hover:translate-x-1">
                {% render 'icon', icon: 'arrow-right', class: 'size-4' %}
              </span>
            </span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Two ways",
  "max_blocks": 2,
  "settings": [
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "The split under the hero, 50/50. Give the two doors different tones: they stack on a phone, and two panels of the same colour touching read as one band." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "What We Do" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "body", "label": "Body" }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "door",
      "name": "Door",
      "settings": [
        { "type": "select", "id": "tone", "label": "Tone", "default": "invert", "options": [
          { "value": "invert", "label": "Espresso" },
          { "value": "brand", "label": "Brown" },
          { "value": "canvas", "label": "Cream" }
        ] },
        { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading" },
        { "type": "textarea", "id": "body", "label": "Description" },
        { "type": "text", "id": "cta", "label": "Link label" },
        { "type": "url", "id": "href", "label": "Link" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Two ways",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "What We Do",
        "body": "We design for everyday wear and beyond. From versatile ready-to-wear collections to custom apparel and merchandise, Wear Label creates thoughtful pieces for individuals, businesses, and communities."
      },
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "door", "settings": {
          "tone": "brand",
          "heading": "Ready to Wear",
          "body": "Thoughtfully designed womenswear for everyday life. Comfortable, versatile, and easy to style — made to become pieces you'll reach for again and again."
        } },
        { "type": "door", "settings": {
          "tone": "canvas",
          "heading": "Custom & Business",
          "body": "Custom apparel and merchandise solutions for companies, organizations, communities, and events. From uniforms to special projects, we create pieces tailored to your needs.",
          "href": "/pages/custom"
        } }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
